  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="5ff99f80-b7af-5370-99c7-efe3a4f81b42" activityNameId="3fd7be4b-0f5c-530e-810d-b008a54b6d64" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Disposal, sludge from FeCl3 production, 30% water, to underground deposit</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Disposal, sludge from FeCl3 production, 30% water, to underground deposit
No emissions from waste material are inventoried. Operators claim absolute long-term safety after closure. Non-zero risk of mine flooding remains.;
Synonyms: sludge from the production of iron chloride; 
UUID: d5be22b0-a997-41ee-9e3a-0523588a0f0c
waste packaging, process material and energy demands for underground disposal in old salt mine (Herfa Neurode, Germany).
Location: Specific to the deposition technology in Herfa-Neurode, Germany, in 2000. Well applicable to modern deposition practices in Europe. Three other underground deposits in old salt mines exist in Germany (Heilbronn, Zielitz, and Borth) and one in France (Wittelsheim).
Technology: Ordered, recorded and potentially retrievable deposition of various types of hazardous wastes (excluding radioactive, explosive, burnable, or infectious wastes)
Time period: undefined
Production volume: Herfa-Neurode processes approximately 100'000 t/a. 
Sampling: Data taken from literature sources and estimates based on information &amp; pictures from the Herfa operators. 
Extrapolations: &lt;null&gt;
Uncertainty adjustments: &lt;null&gt;
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_d5be22b0-a997-41ee-9e3a-0523588a0f0c.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
      <geography geographyId="948c6fdb-25b0-54ae-bf5c-e320176f724c" geographyContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83">
        <shortname xml:lang="en">DE</shortname>
        <comment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Location: Specific to the deposition technology in Herfa-Neurode, Germany, in 2000. Well applicable to modern deposition practices in Europe. Three other underground deposits in old salt mines exist in Germany (Heilbronn, Zielitz, and Borth) and one in France (Wittelsheim).</text>
        </comment>
      </geography>
      <technology>
        <comment>
